course: dictionary
lesson: 1142
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word brick is a good example. «The third little pig built his house of bricks» uses it for the hard blocks that make walls. «You're a brick» uses it for a person who is kind and helpful, which is a very different meaning.
explain: Ask yourself what the sentence is about. If it is about building, a brick is a block. If it is about how a person behaves, look for a meaning about kindness. Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Brick can also be a verb. «They will brick up the gap in the old wall» means they will fill the gap with bricks. The words gap and wall are the clues, so you can work out the meaning even though brick is being used as an action.
fact: A brick is a hard block made of baked clay, used to build walls and houses.
fact: «Brick» can also mean the material itself, as in «a brick house».
fact: A toy brick is a building block that children stack together.
fact: «Brick by brick» means step by step, slowly making something bigger.
fact: In Australia, «a brick short of a load» means someone is a bit silly or mad.
exercise: «The third little pig built his house of bricks.» What is a brick in this sentence? | A toy dog | A sweet, like a chocolate bar | A kind and helpful person | *A hard block made of baked clay, used for building
exercise: «You're a brick, thanks for helping me carry all those boxes.» What does «You're a brick» mean? | Someone who is very angry | *Someone who is very kind and helpful | Someone made of clay | Someone who is very tall
exercise: «They will brick up the gap in the old wall.» What does brick up mean? | To knock the whole wall down | To paint the wall red | *To fill the gap with bricks | To climb over the wall
test: «Running outside during an earthquake is dangerous because of falling glass and bricks.» What are the bricks in this sentence? | *Hard building blocks that could fall and hurt people | Tiny drops of water | Soft sweets that could melt | Sheets of paper blowing in the wind
test: «The farmers built their houses brick by brick, slowly and carefully.» What does «brick by brick» mean? | In a straight line | Very quickly, all at once | Without any help from anyone | *Step by step
exam: Sam says, «It's just bricks and mortar, nothing more.» What does «bricks and mortar» mean? | A house full of toys | *Just a building | A garden with flowers | A museum of history
